<Project>

    <!--
        The version is read out of build.yaml and is not written here. build.yaml is
        the file an installing server reads to decide whether it already has this
        release, so it is the one that has to be right, and a second copy in this file
        is a copy that can disagree with it. A plugin reporting one version to the
        dashboard and another in its package is a support conversation nobody can win.

        The scheme the number follows is written in build.yaml beside the number.
    -->
    <PropertyGroup>
        <WatchlistManifestPath>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)build.yaml</WatchlistManifestPath>
        <WatchlistManifestText>$([System.IO.File]::ReadAllText($(WatchlistManifestPath)))</WatchlistManifestText>
        <WatchlistVersion>$([System.Text.RegularExpressions.Regex]::Match($(WatchlistManifestText), `(?m)(?&lt;=^version:[ \t]*")[^"]*(?="[ \t]*\r?$)`).Value)</WatchlistVersion>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <PropertyGroup>
        <Version>$(WatchlistVersion)</Version>
        <AssemblyVersion>$(WatchlistVersion)</AssemblyVersion>
        <FileVersion>$(WatchlistVersion)</FileVersion>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <!--
        The dependency graph is pinned, and the pin lives here rather than in a
        workflow because the workflow is not where the graph is resolved. Both of the
        gate's build legs call a reusable workflow in another repository, which this
        tree cannot pass a restore flag to, so a rule written into a workflow file
        here would apply to nothing the gate actually runs. A property every project
        inherits applies to every restore of this tree, on the gate, in an editor and
        on a contributor's machine, which is the same rule in one place.

        RestorePackagesWithLockFile writes packages.lock.json next to each project.
        RestoreLockedMode then refuses a restore whose resolved graph differs from
        that file, so a package that silently floats to another version stops the
        build instead of shipping. Two builds of one commit resolving different
        packages is the failure both lines exist against, and it is a failure whose
        whole symptom is that nothing looks wrong.

        Locked mode is on everywhere and not only on the gate, deliberately. A pin
        that binds the gate and not the desk means the first person to meet it is
        whoever opens the pull request, and the message they get is about a machine
        they cannot see. Here the restore that would have changed the graph fails on
        the machine that changed it.

        Changing a dependency on purpose is therefore an explicit act. Run a restore
        of the solution with the force-evaluate switch, which re-resolves the graph
        and rewrites the lock files, and commit them with the change to the package
        reference. A pull request that moves one without the other cannot restore.
        The switch is not spelled here as a literal because a double hyphen cannot
        appear inside an XML comment; the restore names it in full in the NU1004 it
        refuses with, which is where somebody meets this.
    -->
    <PropertyGroup>
        <RestorePackagesWithLockFile>true</RestorePackagesWithLockFile>
        <RestoreLockedMode>true</RestoreLockedMode>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <!--
        Locked mode has one hole, and it was measured rather than guessed at: it applies
        to a lock file that exists. Move the file away and the next restore writes a new
        one from whatever the feed offers that day and reports success, so the way to
        turn the pin off is to delete it. That is also the deletion a reviewer is least
        likely to look at, because the file is generated and long and nobody reads it.

        This target runs before the restore that would rewrite the file and refuses
        instead. Every project in this tree has a lock file today, so the condition is
        false everywhere and this costs nothing until somebody removes one.

        A project added tomorrow has no lock file and meets this error on its first
        restore, which is the intended cost and not an accident. Restore it once with
        the locked-mode property set to false, commit the lock file the restore writes,
        and the target is silent again.
    -->
    <Target Name="RefuseARestoreWithNoLockFileToRestoreAgainst" BeforeTargets="CollectPackageReferences">
        <Error
            Condition="'$(RestoreLockedMode)' == 'true' And !Exists('$(MSBuildProjectDirectory)/packages.lock.json')"
            Text="$(MSBuildProjectName) has no packages.lock.json. Locked mode only binds a lock file that exists, so restoring now would resolve the graph afresh and write a new pin over the one that was removed. Restore this project once with RestoreLockedMode set to false and commit the lock file it writes." />
    </Target>

    <!--
        A version that could not be read leaves those three properties empty, and
        MSBuild then supplies 1.0.0.0 of its own accord. That is the one outcome this
        file must not have: a build quietly stamping a number nobody chose, which
        happens to be the number the upstream template ships. So it stops here instead.
    -->
    <Target Name="RefuseAVersionThatCouldNotBeRead" BeforeTargets="BeforeBuild">
        <Error
            Condition="'$(WatchlistVersion)' == ''"
            Text="No version could be read from $(WatchlistManifestPath). It has to carry a top-level quoted version entry, because every version this build stamps is derived from it." />
    </Target>

</Project>
